latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Background Identification:\n   - Count the occurrences of each color in the input grid.\n   - Identify the most frequent color as the background.\n\n2. Shape Extraction:\n   - Traverse the grid and identify connected regions of non-background colors.\n   - For each shape, store:\n     a) Color\n     b) Dimensions (height and width)\n     c) 2D array representing the shape's structure\n     d) Original position in the input grid\n\n3. Shape Sorting:\n   - Sort the extracted shapes based on:\n     a) Color value (ascending order)\n     b) If colors are equal, sort by area (ascending order)\n     c) If areas are equal, sort by original position in the input grid (top-left to bottom-right)\n\n4. Initial Grid Size Determination:\n   - Calculate the total area of all shapes.\n   - Set initial grid size to the ceiling of the square root of this area.\n\n5. Shape Placement Algorithm:\n   - Start with the smallest grid size that can theoretically fit all shapes.\n   - For each shape in the sorted order:\n     a) Try to place the shape in the top-left most available position.\n     b) If it doesn't fit, try the next available position moving right, then down.\n     c) If the shape doesn't fit anywhere, move to step 6.\n   - If all shapes are placed successfully, move to step 7.\n\n6. Grid Expansion:\n   - If not all shapes could be placed, increase the grid size by 1 in both dimensions.\n   - Clear the grid and return to step 5.\n\n7. Optimization:\n   - Shift all shapes as far up and left as possible without overlapping.\n   - Remove any empty rows at the bottom or empty columns on the right.\n\n8. Validation:\n   - Ensure all non-background colors from the input are present in the output.\n   - Verify that the total area of each color in the output matches the input.\n\n9. Output Generation:\n   - Create a new ColoredGrid object with the optimized arrangement.\n\nImplementation Details:\n\n1. Shape Representation:\n   Create a Shape class with properties: color, height, width, structure (2D array), and original position.\n\n2. Grid Representation:\n   Use a 2D array to represent the working grid during the placement process.\n\n3. Placement Function:\n   Implement a function that tries to place a shape at a given position, checking for conflicts with already placed shapes.\n\n4. Shifting Function:\n   Implement functions to shift shapes up and left, maintaining their relative positions.\n\n5. Area Calculation:\n   Implement a function to calculate the area of each color in both input and output grids for validation.\n\n6. Main Solver Function:\n   Implement the main solver function that orchestrates the entire process, from shape extraction to final output generation.\n\nThis approach should consistently produce outputs that match the expected results for the given examples and generalize well to other potential test cases. It balances the requirements of compactness, shape integrity, and the observed patterns in color and size-based arrangements."
